# LingFine

LingFine is a browser-based linguistic annotation environment for building
richly annotated datasets for languages whose surface forms are shaped by
interacting systems of morphology, tone, and vowel harmony. It is developed
against Igbo and designed to extend to languages with comparable
morphotonological structure.

Annotation runs at three levels. Sentences carry clause type, force, polarity,
tense, aspect, mood, focus, and discourse context. Tokens carry lemmas,
part-of-speech categories, dependency relations, and tonal and ATR features.
Words decompose into prefixes, roots, suffixes, and auxiliaries, each annotated
in its own right.

The framework records why a surface form occurs, not only that it occurs.
Conventional tools capture surface facts as static records. LingFine links
realised forms to their grammatical triggers and derivational processes,
capturing lexical and realised tone, tone-control and ATR-control
relationships, mapping operations, harmony-domain structure, morpheme-internal
composition, and derivation-rule citation.

A single annotation pass yields several resources at once. Export is to CoNLL-U
at two levels: a standard treebank of inter-token dependencies, and an intraword
layer using the multiword-token mechanism for relations between morphemes inside
complex words. Registries for lexical items, morphemes, and derivation rules
build incrementally, with consistency checking and POS-keyed homophone
disambiguation, so the same pass yields a lexicon, a morpheme inventory, and
derivation-rule documentation alongside the treebank.

<!-- SCREENSHOT: the three-level annotation view for a single sentence — caption it to show sentence, token and morpheme panels together -->
![Annotation workspace](docs/images/workspace.png)

## Annotation levels

**Sentence.** Clause type, illocutionary force, polarity, tense, aspect, mood,
focus, and surrounding discourse context are recorded as sentence-scoped
features, establishing the grammatical environment in which token- and
morpheme-level realisations are licensed.

**Token.** Each token carries a lemma, a part-of-speech category, a dependency
relation to its head, and its tonal and ATR features — both the lexical
(underlying) values and the realised (surface) values, so the difference
between them is explicit rather than lost.

**Morpheme.** Complex words decompose into prefixes, roots, suffixes, and
auxiliaries. Each morpheme is annotated in its own right, and the relations
between morphemes inside a word are recorded, so word-internal structure is
first-class rather than collapsed into the token.

<!-- SCREENSHOT: a single complex word decomposed into its morphemes with tone/ATR fields visible — caption it to highlight lexical vs realised tone on one morpheme -->
![Morpheme decomposition](docs/images/morpheme.png)

## Registries and validation

Three registries build incrementally as annotation proceeds:

- **Lexicon** — lexical items keyed for POS so homophones with distinct
  categories are disambiguated rather than merged.
- **Morpheme inventory** — the recurring prefixes, roots, suffixes, and
  auxiliaries encountered, with their attested behaviour.
- **Derivation rules** — documented rules, each with a stable identifier that
  token- and morpheme-level annotations can cite.

Consistency checking runs against these registries during annotation, flagging
divergence between a new annotation and the accumulated record so drift is
caught as it happens rather than in a later audit.

## Export

| Target | Level | Contents |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CoNLL-U (treebank) | Inter-token | Standard dependency treebank: one token per row, with lemma, POS, head, and dependency relation. |
| CoNLL-U (intraword) | Intra-word | Morpheme-level relations inside complex words, using the multiword-token mechanism. |
| Lexicon | Lexical | POS-keyed lexical items with disambiguated homophones. |
| Morpheme inventory | Morphological | Recurring morphemes with attested behaviour. |
| Derivation rules | Derivational | Documented rules with citable identifiers. |
| Phonological inventory | Phonological | Vowel and consonant inventory with the grounding used to resolve ATR and tone claims. |
| Progress (JSON) | All | Complete, self-contained snapshot of the working session for backup and transfer. |

## Downstream uses

The exported resources are intended to support dependency parsing and treebank
research, morphological analysis, tone and ATR modelling, lexicon and
morpheme-inventory construction, and the training and evaluation of models on
languages whose orthography under-represents tone and vowel harmony.

## Repository contents

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `docs/annotation-guide.md` | Feature definitions across the three levels, plus derivation, registries, consistency checking, hard cases, and export mapping. |
| `docs/tone-conventions.md` | Tone notation, lexical vs realised marking, encoding, and Unicode normalisation. |
| `data/phonology.json` | Schema for the phonological inventory. |
| `data/sample.json` | Schema for one annotated record across all three levels. |
| `CHANGELOG.md` | Release history. |
| `CITATION.cff` | Citation metadata. |

